1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,800
Good morning everyone! This is the weekly engineering sync for Project Nimbus.

2
00:00:05,800 --> 00:00:09,900
Today is Thursday, and we have four items on the agenda.

3
00:00:09,900 --> 00:00:18,000
The Kubernetes migration, the PostgreSQL latency issue, the Q3 OKRs, and the hiring update.

4
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:22,400
Priya, do you want to start with the migration?

5
00:00:22,400 --> 00:00:24,600
Sure, thanks Marcus!

6
00:00:24,600 --> 00:00:28,900
So the Kubernetes migration is about 70% done.

7
00:00:28,900 --> 00:00:35,500
We moved the authentication service, and the billing service to the new EKS cluster last week.

8
00:00:35,500 --> 00:00:38,500
The main blocker right now is the Redis cache.

9
00:00:38,500 --> 00:00:46,300
When we deployed it with Helm, the persistent volume claims were failing, because of a misconfigured storage class.

10
00:00:46,300 --> 00:00:54,500
I opened a ticket, Jira number NIM-482, and I expect it to be fixed by Tuesday.

11
00:00:54,500 --> 00:00:59,900
Okay, good. Do we need anything from the DevOps team?

12
00:00:59,900 --> 00:01:00,900
Yes.

13
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:05,960
Yes, we need Thomas to approve the Terraform changes for the VPC peering.

14
00:01:05,960 --> 00:01:12,080
Without that, the pods in the staging namespace cannot reach the legacy database.

15
00:01:12,080 --> 00:01:16,040
I can review the Terraform pull request this afternoon.

16
00:01:16,040 --> 00:01:20,440
I saw it in GitHub yesterday, but I did not have time.

17
00:01:20,440 --> 00:01:26,320
Just to confirm, are we still using the T3 large instances for the worker nodes, or did

18
00:01:26,320 --> 00:01:29,120
we switch to Graviton?

19
00:01:29,120 --> 00:01:31,160
We switched to Graviton.

20
00:01:31,160 --> 00:01:39,200
The ARM-based instances are about 20% cheaper, and our Docker images now build for both architectures.

21
00:01:39,200 --> 00:01:40,200
Great.

22
00:01:40,200 --> 00:01:45,520
Let's move to item 2, the PostgreSQL latency issue.

23
00:01:45,520 --> 00:01:48,160
Thomas, what did you find?

24
00:01:48,160 --> 00:01:55,240
So, the P99 latency on the order endpoint went from about 80 milliseconds to almost

25
00:01:55,320 --> 00:01:59,160
400 milliseconds after the release on the 12th.

26
00:01:59,160 --> 00:01:59,920
Ironic, ple-

27
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:06,160
analyze on the slope queries and the problem is a missing composite index on the orders table

28
00:02:06,160 --> 00:02:12,720
on customer id and created that. Someone dropped it during the schema migration with Alembic.

29
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:16,160
Ouch! Is there a fix ready?

30
00:02:17,440 --> 00:02:22,480
Yes. I wrote a new migration that recreates the index concurrently,

31
00:02:22,480 --> 00:02:27,840
so it will not lock the table. I also added a check in our CI pipeline,

32
00:02:27,840 --> 00:02:35,440
using pgstat statements, so we get an alert in metadog if any query goes above 200 milliseconds.

33
00:02:36,720 --> 00:02:44,320
That's really helpful. From the product side, customers definitely noticed. We had 11 support

34
00:02:44,320 --> 00:02:50,480
tickets about slow checkout in the last week, and our NPS score dropped by 3 points.

35
00:02:50,480 --> 00:02:59,280
Thanks, Aisha. Okay, item 3, the Q3OKRs. Aisha, can you give us the summary?

36
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:05,000
Sure. We have three key results for this quarter.

37
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:10,000
First, reduce checkout abandonment from 18% to 12%.

38
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:16,000
We are currently at 14%, so we are on track, but not finished.

39
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:23,000
Second, launch the new recommendation engine, which uses the embedding model from the data science team.

40
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:32,000
That one is delayed, because the vector database, we chose Pinecone, needs a security review from the compliance team.

41
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:39,000
And third, achieve SOC 2 type 2 readiness. We are about halfway there.

42
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:43,000
What is the risk on the SOC 2 work?

43
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:51,000
The main risk is the audit logging. We need every access to customer data to be logged and retained for one year.

44
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:56,000
Right now we only keep logs in CloudWatch for 30 days.

45
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:00,000
We could export the logs to S3 with a live site.

46
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:05,800
policy and move them to Glacier after 90 days. That would be cheap and it would

47
00:04:05,800 --> 00:04:13,280
meet the retention requirement. Perfect, let's take that offline and write it up.

48
00:04:13,280 --> 00:04:20,240
Last item, hiring. We have two open roles, a senior back-end engineer with Go and

49
00:04:20,240 --> 00:04:26,200
gRPC experience and a machine learning engineer for the recommendation work. We

50
00:04:26,200 --> 00:04:31,640
have three candidates in the final round. Priya and Thomas, please submit your

51
00:04:31,640 --> 00:04:38,840
interview feedback in Greenhouse by Friday. We'll do. Yes, I will have mine

52
00:04:38,840 --> 00:04:45,720
done tomorrow. Great. To summarize the action items, Priya will fix the redeemed

53
00:04:45,720 --> 00:04:51,000
storage class issue by Tuesday. Thomas will review the Terraform pull request

54
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:56,880
today and deploy the index migration. Aisha will schedule the Pinecon security

55
00:04:56,880 --> 00:05:01,800
review and draft the log retention plan with.

56
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:05,000
And everyone, please send your greenhouse feedback by Friday.

57
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:08,000
Thanks everyone, see you next week!
